{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pl-08-consignments-partial-lots-weighted-profit",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Partial Lots, Consignments, and Weighted Profit Targets",
    "description": "Combine portions of a stock sold under different margins to find total profit, cost, or an unknown allocation.",
    "teachingApproach": "Develop the commercial relationship step by step, explain why each rate uses its base, show intermediate amounts, check signs and units, then practice transfer with misconception-specific retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12 “Profit and Loss,” printed pages 374–425. Approved scope: Represent each portion's cost as its share of the full consignment, apply its own gain/loss factor, and add resulting receipts. Solve for total CP from a stated overall profit amount, an unknown portion cost, or a target aggregate margin. Teach weights and distinguish fraction of stock from fraction of cost if unit costs differ. Verify component shares sum to the total and the final net profit matches the stated amount. Use solved examples 28–29 and objective patterns on multiple lots."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Partial Lots, Consignments, and Weighted Profit Targets: Combine portions of a stock sold under different margins to find total profit, cost, or an unknown allocation. We establish the model, explain each step, and practice with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A consignment may be divided into portions sold at different rates. If the portions have the same unit cost, their quantity shares are also their CP shares; otherwise compute each cost separately.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let total CP be C. If two-thirds sells at 5% gain and one-third at 2% loss, total gain is (2/3)(5%)C−(1/3)(2%)C= (8/3)% of C.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the currency profit is known, divide that amount by the combined profit fraction to recover total CP. Keep the fraction exact to avoid rounding a small share.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For example, when the weighted net rate is 8/3% and total profit is ₹400, C=400/(8/300)=₹15,000.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A different task gives the full CP and asks for an unknown portion split. Represent the split with x, write gains and losses on each portion, and equate to the stated total result.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Do not apply a single margin to the entire stock when different fractions receive different selling terms. Compute each portion’s SP and add receipts.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If lots have different purchase costs per unit, quantity shares are not CP shares. Calculate the cost of each lot before weighting its rate.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A known overall gain can constrain the unknown cost allocation between two goods. The equation combines profit on one share and loss on the other.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Example: horse and carriage total ₹3,000; horse gains 20%, carriage loses 10%, and overall gain is 2%. Solve 0.2x−0.1(3,000−x)=60 to get horse CP ₹1,200.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The equation’s right-hand side is total gain on the full CP, not the gain on just one portion. Keep that distinction visible.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A stock may be partly sold at a gain and partly at loss. If all cost shares sum to total CP, the weighted result should reconcile exactly with total SP−total CP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check shares: portions must add to 1, costs must add to the stated total, and no portion should be counted twice or omitted.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the unsold fraction remains valuable, it is inventory rather than a completed sale. Only include a sale value if the problem gives one; do not invent a loss on stock still held.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The overall percentage always uses aggregate CP. A weighted average of the rates works only when the weights are each portion’s share of total cost.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A final verification can use a convenient total such as ₹300 or ₹1,500, then compare the computed profit fraction with the stated currency amount.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Name the cost, receipt, and percentage base. Choose an operation that follows the transaction wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
